editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:183:Cael knew the leaf, for it was the one with the noun on it, underlined once, and beneath that five counts and a distance, and she had carried it in her coat for nine days. It was a good deal fuller now than when he had last been shown it; she had ruled a line across under the *110*, and below the line her small exact hand went on to the foot of the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:213:"*Not accounted for: 110 yards. The hour.*" She read the last line more quietly. "*Evidence against: none, which I don't trust.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:109:She had made a table of it in her exact hand. At the head stood the word, underlined once, and under the word his five counts in the order he had given them: *keeps station at forty-five yards; makes no sound; follows the buckle with its head; will not cross the gravel fan; answers nothing, having been given nothing.* Beneath those stood the figure, *110 yards*, and after it, in smaller writing, *gate to stone; C.'s count, paced at a yard to the stride; not taped.* She had gone out in the heat of noon and walked the same hundred and ten yards along the inside of the fence, in her own shorter steps, so as to know in her legs how far it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:Every name on the board in Ardenmere had carried letters after it, and a number: *Cu 3*, and the like. They had been ratings, borrowed from the registry's tiers and kept in somebody's private ledger, and a stranger could not have stood in that yard for a minute without being told where he stood among them. He had learned to read a card by them before he learned anything else; and here there was only a job, and whoever wanted it done, and what they would pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:55:*Five rows,* she wrote instead, and ruled them by touch. *K. L. B. S. O. Each: gone at the stone, going east. Back at the stone, coming west. One stride. Observed by all five at once on the 95th; by me alone, nine times, that night.* And under that, because she kept that sort of account as well: *I am frightened by how well it agrees. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:They stood round it, and it was a handsome thing. The registry had printed it from a plate, in black, on a sheet as long as Brom's arm, and the whole of a continent was there: every district with its bound ruled round it, and every chartered road a firm double line, and the towns in three sizes of letter by their standing. Cael found Ardenmere, and a river he had crossed on a bridge, and a bluff above Ostrand with a small square on it. Somebody had written *ridge* beside a pass in the east, in pencil, and a little farther on, *Shelf; a pine; water at 36*, in the same small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:"I have a row to amend," she said. Her voice was whole. "*Stated by him, not tested by me* stands, since it is still true. I am adding beneath it: *tested by him, at noon on the 96th, unwitnessed; told on the 110th*. And now I want something that I have never had. I have had that hillside for fourteen nights in five heads, and not one account of it on paper. I want what each of you saw. In the order in which you saw it. Nothing that you supposed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:91:She drew a line under the figure. *Counted on the 103rd. One site. Not to be given out as the number of kinds.* And beneath that, since she kept that account too: *I want very much to give it out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:39:Then he reached up, and took hold of the tack in the slip that said *HOLD*, and worked it out of the oak.
