47: "I was the one who didn't know what she was looking at," said Oryn. "A boy went sideways out of a file with a crack like a whip, in a place where I couldn't count to four. Then there was fire in his fist. I've been told since what that was. I'll say what I know, which is less than any of you."
49: She held her two palms up to the lantern.
51: "My hands stop at that line. Yours don't. I had my hands in you for twenty minutes and I can tell you what I didn't find, which is a Path. That's all I've got."
53: Her eyes came to Cael then, and rested on him for the length of a breath. He had watched her cross a grey stone eight times at dawn, alone, and had shut a book on it; and he saw that she knew he had kept it shut.

### ch46:59
55: "Walk away."
57: She left it there, so that nobody should mistake it for the start of something longer.
59: "He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time. There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
61: "And the circles?" said Karis.
63: "I'm coming to them. You said four wheres. One's walked. One's this, with a paper nailed across it." She nodded at the sheets on Seln's knee. "The third's a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and nobody's put a price on it at all. So we go and pace that one, on our own time, with a healer at the edge. I'm not saying leave them alone. I'm saying I'll not walk into the one that somebody's paying us to walk into."
65: She looked down at her sling, and up again.

### ch46:119
115: "You'd walk us onto that floor to get a look at somebody."
117: "I should walk myself onto it. I have told you what I think the paper is. I did not say that it was wise to answer it. I was asked what I would do, and I have said. I should be sorry to have it counted for more than one hand."
119: Karis had opened her mouth and shut it again, and Cael was glad of it. Whatever lay behind those few words was Seln's, and he had laid on the table as much of it as he chose.
121: It was Cael's turn, and he had had the whole of the walk down from the inn to find a reason; he had found several, and he used none of them, since he had learned at an inn's boards what his reasons were worth when he dressed them.
123: "I'll say the thing that none of you can say for me. Then I'll say why it matters."
125: He looked round the four of them, one face at a time.

### ch48:241
237: ---
239: The quiet one did not let any of them sleep until he had said his order, and he said it at the fire, when it was full dark, with the six of them close about it.
241: "I have walked the ruts," said Seln. "All the way round, at dusk. It is sixteen hundred paces and a little more. I found no person and no sign of one, and I shall walk it again before light. That is a fact about the ruts. If I had laid this, I should not be on the ruts tonight either. I should be a quarter of a mile back from them, on my face, in grass of this colour, and I should come up to them when you were over that stone and not before. So I shall tell you what I can see, which is nothing. And I shall tell you what I should assume, which is that they are here."
243: "All of that, and we go," said Brom.
245: "We ruled it at a fire. I am saying how."
247: He gave it to them a piece at a time, as he had once given five people the order of a watch.

### ch51:173
169: "I tied those to be read. There is a stem of grass laid under every knot, and each is where I laid it." He set the lamp down. "Six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it to look into a mule's load."
171: "What did they see?" said Cael. "Tell me as you'd write it for them."
173: "A fence that failed at dusk, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place; and directly afterward a boy, who stood up out of the old road beside that bar and declared. Nobody on that rim saw anything between the stair and the bar. I asked Lira what she saw from the thirty-fourth step, and she says that she saw a stair. They will write that he crept up a rut in bad light while they were watching a hole, and that a knot tied a hand's breadth from such an edge is a knot which nobody should have trusted. A man who lays fences for his living would far sooner put that on paper than the other thing, and he has no cause to think of the other thing at all. It holds."
175: He turned, and went up the old road to the brow by himself.
177: When he came down he had a crossbow in his hands.
179: He brought it into the lamplight and turned it over, slowly, as he turned over any paper. It was a good bow, heavier than the healer's, with a prod of horn and sinew, and its string hung cut from one end. He ran his thumb along the underside of the stock, and under the stirrup, and across the butt.

### ch53:351
347: "There," she said, and let go of Brom's wrist.
349: They made their camp at the track's end, a few yards outside the grey stone, with the two mules hobbled on the rise. It had come on cold again with the dusk. Seln lit the fire, and the six of them sat close about it while Karis said what she wanted.
351: "The walls are cut on the inner side. I could see the courses through the gap, and I could not have read one stroke of them at thirty yards if I had sat on that kerb for a week. So it is the inside or it is nothing."
353: "There's a way to do it," said Seln. "It has given us its rule, and a rule is a plan. On the floor, we move. We rest only on what comes up out of the old work: the path, and the kerb, and the tops of those walls, which are a yard through. A wall-top is a kerb set on end." He counted it off. "We go in at first light. Lira carries the bow, spanned, since she can loose it and cannot bend it. I carry the hook and the sleeve. Karis has her paper and nothing else. Thirty yards of plates, at a walk, with nobody standing; in at the gap; and up."
355: "And me," said Brom.
357: "You'll lift nothing," said Oryn.

### ch55:129
125: "Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
127: "It's not coming out of that water. He read the mud."
129: "I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much. I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
131: He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
133: It was the middle of the afternoon when he saw the other thing, and he saw it only because Brom was making a face at him. Brom lay on his back by the near fire, under two coats, with his right arm strapped to him in every strip of linen that the pannier held. He had his left forefinger laid across his lips, and with his eyes he was pointing at what sat beside him.
135: The healer was asleep.

### ch60:25
21: She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
23: "Fifty-two," said Lira, from the door. She had counted them as they went down.
25: "Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it. I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
27: She set her lamp in the middle, and lit it with a point, and unrolled the hide.
29: Cael did not go to the hearth with the others. He sat down at the meal-sack end of that table, with Hesk's book, while she worked.
31: *Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*

### ch60:73
69: He read it standing in the snow.
71: An old man in Denvash had cut that leaf from his pad a long while ago, and had rolled a ruler in it for a boy who was going where there were no instruments. It had lain in a bag on the wrong side of a river for a month and more. And in that time the boy had walked off the end of another person's brass for three days and come to a marsh, and had stood at a fence of iron with a line going through it. He had not written one word of that west. The old man could not have known that there was a line. He had only known the boy.
73: "He says that it is true to a hair," said Karis, at his elbow. "I should like to see it against mine."
75: "Tonight. I've two letters to write, and that boat's not going to wait for me."
77: He wrote them at the end of Pike's plank, on two leaves torn out of Hesk's own book, with the horn ink-pot and a borrowed pen. He had owed both for a long while, and he had been waiting for a thing to say; and he had not expected, when it came, that it would go into so few words.
79: *Nineteen, past the Line, and there's a roof. It's an inn. The shape you said to mind is a line, and I've got the tool for it now. — C.*

