===== CHAPTER 36 SPEECH =====
9: "She runs the long reading at first light," said Cael. "On me, in the back room, with the door shut."
13: "She called it that at Oxhollow. Both hands, and all of her, for minutes, with the one under her hands lying still and whole. You were all in that yard, and I saw four people hear it."
15: "We heard it," said Brom.
19: "And none of you said what it was, and nor did I. I've had fifteen days in which to say it, and a ring; and I'm saying it now with one night to go, which is late." He looked at the flame of Karis's lantern. She had held up one finger when she lit it, to show him how much oil an hour at a stone had left her, and he meant to have said all of it before that was gone. "It's the thing that I take them in."
23: "I've nine, and you know how each of them came. There was somebody with a Path, who used it on me or against me for long enough, and meant it; and there was something that mattered to both of us in how it came out, whether that was a card, or a ruling, or only which of us was left standing. That's the whole of what any of them had in common, and Karis has a page on it."
29: "So I've laid tomorrow beside her page, a piece at a time. It wants to be sustained. She says twenty minutes, with both her hands on me, and I've never stood a bout that was a quarter as long. It wants stakes, on both sides. Mine are that I'm three days off that floor, with every one of the nine still shaken by it. Hers are that she's spent three mendings on us and she's late at the top of her round, and she means to lay out a fourth on a boy who has given her nothing four times. And it wants to be in earnest."
31: "On you," said Lira. "Not against you."
35: "On me. She'll go into it with everything that she is, since that's the only way she does anything."
37: "That's the part that's never been so," said Brom slowly. "The others were all trying to put you on the floor. Even the captain was."
39: "Ephram was trying to beat me. He wasn't trying to hurt me, and the notice knew the difference; it said so, in two words that I'd never seen on one."
53: "Why've you pulled them out?" said Lira.
59: "Is it right, though?" said Brom. "Her model."
61: "She doesn't know, and I don't, since there's been nothing to try it on. Every one of the nine came out of a fight of some kind. There's never been a person who did a real thing to me for a long time, at a cost, who wasn't standing on the other side of a floor." He touched the second leaf. "If she's right, tomorrow is the first morning of my life on which it could happen with nobody struck. And if it does, I shall have a piece of what a healer reads with."
65: "It isn't the model that I wanted you for. It's what's under it. She doesn't know what I am."
69: "I've told her nothing. She has put her hands on me four times and found no banks, and she watched me go up a hill with fire in my fist, and in all that time she has never asked me one question that wasn't about a hurt. She thinks that she is going to lay me on a table and read me. If something is taken from her while she does it, she'll not have been told that it might be. She can't say yes to it, and she can't say no."
73: "Then tell her," said Lira. "Tonight. Before."
77: "She's across a yard, and there's a door. You knock on it, and you say that there's a thing you do, and what it is, and that it might do it to her. And then she says what she says."
79: "I've been at that since the fork in the road," said Cael. "I'll give you what I've got, and there are three reasons."
81: "Go on."
83: "The first is that if I tell her, it's a different morning. She would be reading a patient that she'd been warned about. She might keep some of herself back, to see; or she might go in looking for the very thing I'd named. I don't believe that either of those is the whole of her, and the page says that it has to be real. I'd have turned it into a trial."
87: "The second is that it isn't all mine to tell. I can't say what I am without saying that I've nine, and where a thing of that kind comes from; and then she is within one question of the one that's sealed. She talks to every carter on seven roads. She would never mean any harm by it."
89: "No," said Seln.
93: "I shall not be a reason. You shut that thing for my sake, and you have kept it shut at a cost that I watched you pay in a river. But I have had four nights in which to consider who was looking in a lane, and I find that I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant. Strike the second. It is not available to you."
97: "Struck," he said, and his voice was not quite steady.
99: "That leaves the first and the third," said Lira. "What's the third?"
101: "That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
103: "I said something," said Lira. "In a lane, when I'd never heard of it either. You told me on the day you knew, and I didn't run."
105: "You had watched it happen. You'd felt it go."
107: "She's felt you not be there, four times, and that's more than I had." She leaned forward, as far as the sling would let her. "Those are reasons, and they're good ones; you never bring bad ones. Which of them is the real one?"
111: "I don't know what she'd say."
115: "If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
119: "That's honest," said Brom. "I'll say so much for it."
123: "What's it cost her?"
125: "I don't know."
127: "You must know something of it. You've done it nine times."
