101: "A person who lets himself down off a wall stands for as long as it takes him to find his feet. It took a man at three, and he had been walking the breath before." Seln's mild eyes went across the water. "And that man has one arm to come down with."
103: "So we sit," said Brom.
105: "We sit until it goes back to its round. I cannot tell you how long that will be."
115: "You're copying," she said.
117: "I'm not getting another chance," said Karis, from under the edge.
119: "No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
131: "Stay where you are," said Cael.
133: "I'm a cart's length from the rest of you, on a thing that I can't get off. If it comes on to freeze, I'll be here till the thaw." Brom was turning himself about. "It's a step. I've come down worse with a box on."
135: "You had two arms."
137: "I've a good one."
151: "Brom," said Lira. There was no answer. "*Brom.*"
169: "It has heard him fall," said Seln.
195: "One," said Seln's voice, above him. "Two."
201: "Three."
227: "Out," said Lira's voice, cracking. "It's coming up. Cael, get *out*—"
245: "Brom," Cael said. "Wall. Your weight. Now."
263: "In," said Karis. She had her chin on the stone and both arms over her sheets, and she had left off copying at last.
267: "Span," said Lira.
277: "I've lost it," she said. "It's on your side. You've the line."
283: "Three," said Lira. She was calling them for him, as a lean man had once called hers. "Low. It throws low."
287: "Four. Keep the last."
291: "They're on the sill," said Lira. "Both of them. She's got him sitting."
301: "It's alive," said Lira.
303: "It is alive," said Seln. He was coming back along the wall with the bow. "And it is going down, and it is hurt, and I do not know how long any of those will last. Karis. Paper. We walk."
307: "I spent your week," said Brom. His face was the colour of the wall.
309: "You spent it in a morning. I said that you would." She had her shoulder under his left arm, and she was wet to the waist. "Walk. You can be sorry on the grass."
317: "One," he said.
321: "What did we get?"
323: "Walls," Karis said, from the coping. "I've the walls. Twelve sheets."

