chapter-01.md:73:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It had looked like a thread from the ridge. It was a river, forty feet of fast brown water over stones, the colour of weak tea, and so loud after the silence of the hill that Lira had to say everything twice. Trees stood along it, which he had seen from above as a darkness; and they were a darkness still, tall and close, with bark as red as a fox and a smell he could put no name to.
chapter-05.md:111:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He had wire. They got a pole under the axle, and Brom lifted, with the cords standing in his neck and no grey in him anywhere, while Seln and Cael built stones up under the bed. He whittled a new pin out of thorn with the hedging bill, and bound the split rim round and round, and twisted the wire tight with the back of the blade. Karis timed him, since nobody had told her not to.
chapter-05.md:235:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:*It tracked the buckle.* He had that much, and no more. At forty-five yards the grey head had moved when the buckle moved, and had lost it when the buckle stopped; a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth had drawn no answer that Cael could see. He could not say what sense had found the buckle, and the finding told him nothing of what the thing was waiting for.
chapter-06.md:33:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
chapter-06.md:131:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He stood in the grass for some time with that.
chapter-06.md:227:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The carter had said it as he might have said that the hay was in. *That's the season open.* He had meant the roads, and the weather on them, and a board in a meadow with slips enough on it for every crew that could walk; and he could not have known what the word had been to the person he said it to.
chapter-07.md:305:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Three. Whole, and fed, and none of them carrying anything they had not set out with.
chapter-08.md:263:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Cael watched her come down it and thought of a painted floor at Norhold. She had been quicker there, with the burst, and she had never looked like this, for there she had been running at a mark. Here she ran thirty yards of loose stones for no one, and stopped because she had decided to.
chapter-10.md:201:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was a good deal bigger than the one in front of her, and one of its ears hung in two pieces. It was not looking at her, and that was the part she would remember afterward, when she remembered any of it. It looked past her shoulder, up the bar, at nothing, with its weight forward on its toes. It had been lying on that spot when she ran at it yesterday. It had stood there for the whole of this, and a mild man had told her in a loft, in so many words, exactly where it would be and what it was for.
chapter-11.md:83:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He made himself think it through to the end, since nobody else was placed to.
chapter-11.md:101:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It had turned its head for a knife's pommel and turned it again for Iron Skin, and it had not taken a step. It was the cut dog, and things came to it; they ran from the beaches, and it stood at the end of where they ran. It was waiting now, at the tail, for exactly that.
chapter-11.md:175:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He felt the weight of it, like a door shutting on his leg, and no more than that.
chapter-14.md:121:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was one place, on a sheet of sailcloth in an inn; and Cael thought that he had never in his life gone up anything by a single rung and known so exactly what the rung was made of.
chapter-14.md:209:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:They stirred it, and it was the most foolish-looking work he had ever been paid for.
chapter-14.md:271:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He looked at the candle, which burned in its chink as it had burned all morning. The flame was perhaps a little longer than it had been, and a little blue about the root; or perhaps it was not, and it told him nothing that he could have sworn to.
chapter-14.md:319:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:They stirred the shaft again, and worked it after that in turns of a hundred, by Karis's count, and Seln had his way after all and took two of them. He set the six stones back in their course, hanging in the loop with his knees against the wall. They drew eighty buckets before the water came up clear, and Brom drank the first cup, since somebody had to.
chapter-14.md:369:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He counted. He was doing it before he knew, as he counted everything, the strides and the breaths between them; and by the end of the first sixty yards he had the whole of it. Her left leg did what it had always done. Her right came down a little short, and the push from it arrived a shade behind where it ought to be, so that every second stride began a little after she had meant it to.
chapter-15.md:239:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:They came round the bend and saw what he was standing in front of.
chapter-18.md:39:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The shelf under his boots was whole. But at the very rim, where the dark rib had weathered, the stone had come away underneath in a long hollow, and what was left on top was a lip no thicker than a plank, with daylight below it. A wheel that strayed nine inches to the right would be standing on that.
chapter-20.md:311:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:"Can I stand on it?" said Lira. She had been flexing the foot all this while, a little at a time, with the wonder of a person who has been given back a thing she had already begun to grieve for.
chapter-22.md:147:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She was not a person who guessed. She had been taught by a woman who struck her knuckles for guessing, and she had come to be glad of it. So she went over what her hands had found, as she would have gone over a patient for a second healer, and she kept strictly to that.
chapter-22.md:183:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She gave that hold a night and a morning, and went on. They had put a loaf and a cold fowl in the off pannier without being asked, as they did every round, and the smith's girl had looked at the near hind hoof. It was how the coat had come to her. Nobody at any of the seven gates had ever spoken of a price, and nobody had ever let her leave with less than she came with.
chapter-22.md:215:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She walked a mile on that.
chapter-22.md:217:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She was not a person who wanted things. She had wanted one thing, once, which was to keep a list in the right order, and she had got it, and it had filled her days so full that there was no room left over. She had not thought that there was anything else her hands were hungry for.
chapter-22.md:313:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Cael went through it for the place where he could say so. He thought of a smith in Lowmarch, and six marks owed, and a tail-board that had not banged. He thought of what a ford-keeper had counted out, and what the ford-keeper had said he was paying for.
chapter-22.md:315:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He had no argument. The man had not said that they had failed. He had said what the bench was, on a board that wrote down outcomes, and it was that.
chapter-23.md:9:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:There were three boxes, and they stood on the bare ground by a gate-house wall. That was the item he liked least. A hundred and sixteen marks lay in Karis's purse, and six of them were a smith's. The plank where those six would be paid was six days down a road, and each box weighed as much as a grown sheep. He had one man who was allowed to carry, and four who were not fit to.
chapter-23.md:35:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He asked them in the yard, by the well, with the light gone grey. Brom looked at three boxes and then at his own left wrist, and Cael watched him weigh a road of six days against a rule of three, and put his hand up. Lira's was up already. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. They had to wake Karis for hers, and she raised it before her eyes were properly open and asked afterward what she had voted for.
chapter-23.md:187:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was the fan again, Cael thought, and he was glad to see that it was the fan made small; there was no slope in it and nothing overhead. But somebody had to learn which logs were sound before a wheel was trusted to any of them, and there was only one way that the five of them had ever done that.
chapter-23.md:213:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He carried it the whole way over. He came back for the left-hand one, and then for the right; and when Seln stepped forward for the third, since three crossings of that moss was a long way under a box, Brom shook his head and took that too. The carter watched him go by each time with a hundredweight in his arms and a look of deep content on his plain face, and plainly could not make out what he was seeing. Carrying boxes was only what a big man was for.
chapter-24.md:135:\(.*$:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
chapter-25.md:233:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The eleventh was at Pike's elbow. He was a thin young man with ink on his cuffs and a ledger under his arm, and he had come up from the landing at a trot when he was sent for.
chapter-28.md:45:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was very tidy, and she made herself look at that, because it was what she was afraid of.
chapter-28.md:145:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:They went on. Cael walked the fourth cord with his head turned back over his shoulder. The bar stood in the grass where they had left it, brown and small, beside a square of black turf; and he thought that it was a strange thing to have crossed a Line, and a mountain, and ninety-six days of road, and to be met at the far end of all of it by two numerals that somebody at a counter had signed for.
chapter-29.md:225:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The wind went over the grass. Nobody at the stone had anything to put beside that.
chapter-30.md:67:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Cael came and looked. All along the brink, as far as he could follow it to either hand, the floor turned up and rolled over on itself, smooth, with no corner anywhere, so that its crest stood a finger above the grey within and a hand above the turf outside. He had stepped over that a quarter of an hour ago and never marked it. A person running would have had to.
chapter-30.md:153:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Oryn had sat down on the western lip with her boot off. She had watched the whole afternoon from there, pressing the strip on her heel now and then with her thumb, and she had not once asked what any of it was for.
chapter-31.md:145:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:They were at the south. Fifteen yards on along the kerb two grey shapes were standing on the grass, shoulder to shoulder, with their heads level. They had gone ahead on the white and dropped off the rim where there was nobody under it with a pole, and they stood now on turf, where a hound has all the spring that it was born with.
chapter-32.md:37:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
chapter-32.md:93:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was cut off almost as it began. It was still the loudest sound that anybody had made on that ground since the six of them crossed a stone; and not one head in the grass on either side so much as turned to it. They did not hunt by that.
chapter-32.md:131:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The jaws came open by the width of two fists, and Karis had them out before he could have told her to.
chapter-32.md:279:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:At ten feet from the stone the last of the nine told him what it had been looking for.
chapter-32.md:311:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was full dark. The stars were out. He lay where he had fallen and went over it, since somebody had to.
chapter-33.md:267:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The seven did not come, and the stone lay where it had lain, and behind it, a long way off up the slope, there was a faint paleness that he would not have seen if he had not known where to look. He watched the stars go over, and tried to make the sentence that she had given him leave to wait for.
chapter-35.md:155:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:They walked west on the ninety-eighth, and Lira went the whole way on the flat of her foot, since she had been told to.
chapter-35.md:165:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was the arm that she could not get used to.
chapter-35.md:191:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She stopped it. She did not know afterward how. It was like stopping dead off a full run at the lip of a gravel bar: there was all that going in her, and then there were two feet. She shut the hand and jammed the elbow back against her ribs, and she turned her right shoulder in, hard, the wrong way round, and took Karis on that.
chapter-35.md:273:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
chapter-35.md:277:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She would lay both hands on him in the morning, and all that she was, for as long as it took. She would do it in earnest, since she did nothing in any other way. He had felt that ninth thing come to him at the end of a bout with a friend who had asked only to be shown straight; he knew, as well as he knew anything about himself, what her morning was likely to be made of.
chapter-35.md:281:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The second thing took him the rest of the mile. A woman was walking on the low side of a mule, six feet from him, who had added a stop to the only thing she owned in order to read a boy whom she could not read; and she had not the least idea that the boy might come away from her table with a piece of what she read with.
chapter-36.md:253:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She had told him in a yard to hold nothing. He had spent a hundred days in learning to refuse the nine, a stride at a time; and now he was to lie here for twenty minutes and neither refuse them nor let them go, and he did not know the name of that.
chapter-38.md:7:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Her hands lay where they had lain for twenty minutes, the left under his neck and the right on the bone, over the very place that he was speaking of.
chapter-39.md:65:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He did not sleep that night, and he had not expected to.
chapter-39.md:123:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was the last of the year's. The carters had come up through the stretch at noon the day before and slept in the cart-yard, three heavy men in sheepskins, with frost on their mules' eyelashes; and they wished to be unloaded and gone before the roads grew any harder. Holds farther up the valley would fetch that barley through the whole of the cold term, a cart at a time. Until then it lay in Thornwater's loft, under Thornwater's thatch, which was all that a grain hold was for.
chapter-40.md:89:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Brom held the basket out to her, as a man holds out a hand of cards that he is sure of.
chapter-40.md:153:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He did it as he did everything. Each bowl received its portion, and Cael could not have told one from another by weight if he had had Karis's brass to do it with.
chapter-40.md:173:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:She said it, in a creak, with great exactness, all four lines, in a language that not one of them understood; and at the end of it there was a respectful hush, into which the small man at the spit said that he hoped it was not a curse, since he had the gravy to think of.
chapter-41.md:207:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It had come to her in her sleep, whole, as the answer to a long sum sometimes comes when a person has stopped doing it. She had lain between two sacks of barley with her eyes open and looked at it. Then she had got up, very quietly, and come down the ladder with the lantern unlit, and lit it at the trestle with a point. It was the first that she had set since a hillside, and she had been glad to have a hand to reach with.
chapter-42.md:147:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:So that was how it would be. She would never loose a bolt that somebody else had not bent his back for.
chapter-42.md:295:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It was her bad foot. She saw that, and she saw in the next breath that it did not matter, since the right foot was to be set down whole, heel and toe together, and to roll nowhere and push at nothing, exactly as a healer had ordered it at a stone. It was the first thing that she had been asked to do with that ankle in twelve days that the ankle was good for.
chapter-44.md:155:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:There was light enough from the frost. He opened the book on the well's rim, at the leaf where an inventory ended, and wetted the pencil, and wrote it under her eyes without hurrying. He had written a leaf once in a loft so that he could never claim to have been surprised, and it had permitted nothing; he wrote this one so that there should be a line in his own hand that he could be held to.
chapter-45.md:187:\(.*$:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Stair road. South of Lowmarch; the first day; where the road drops off the ridge and the ground opens. Three hundred marks (three hundred).*
chapter-45.md:199:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He did not raise his voice for it, and he kept his eyes on the book. He had told a man in this room, on the eighty-ninth evening, that he would have done the same with the first of those papers; and on the ninetieth he had held the first out across his plank to five people and written five strokes on it. He had not said then what he would do in their place. Cael understood that he had been given something that Pike did not give, and that it had not been asked for.
chapter-46.md:309:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Then he audited his vote, since he owed that.
chapter-48.md:83:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He got up on his knees. He laid his left palm on the back of her neck, under the cropped hair, where her own left hand had once lain under his for twenty minutes, and took her wrist again; and he felt her draw one long breath and let the whole of it go, as she had taught him to.
chapter-49.md:187:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:There was a scrape of steel, and a spark, and another. Karis's breath went in and out somewhere to the left. Cael knew what she was doing as well as if he had seen her. She had put out her hand in the dark to set a point on her own wick, as she had done every night of her life since she was fourteen; and there was no hand to reach with.
chapter-50.md:63:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The white lasted a long time, and they filled it with small things, since there was nothing else to fill it with.
chapter-50.md:101:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He sat down beside her, since there was nothing that he could say to it. Her hand found his sleeve in the dark, and patted it twice, clumsily, as she might have patted a book that she meant to come back to.
chapter-50.md:175:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:His voice was as mild as ever; and Cael, who had waited all through that reckoning for somebody else to say it, saw now that it could not have come from anybody else in the room, since it was the giver's to say what a gift was for.
chapter-50.md:291:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He told it not to.
chapter-51.md:55:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:Then Compression, which a fighter had put through him on a circuit, long ago, arrived and did what it was for.
chapter-54.md:185:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:It had left its round for a stone on a plate. It had left everything, yesterday, for a point that would not come away. What would it leave a fallen man for?
chapter-54.md:265:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:The whole heap of it shuddered, and the walls shook under them, and it did not let go. Cael felt the shudder come up through the point. He held. He had nothing else to do, and nothing else to do it with.
chapter-56.md:143:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He had told himself at a grey stone that a captain who asks a question he has no column for has only given himself something more to carry. It had been sound, and he had had three to carry then. He had four now. And he was proposing to stand on some floor beside that boy after the thaw, if the boy went where he seemed to be going. A man does not walk his people up to a thing that he has been too careful to ask the name of.
chapter-58.md:161:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He could have gone over. He knew only that he had crossed three such lines before and that the fragments had answered there; nobody had tested this line, and nobody would. He kept his boots beside theirs. It was a crew's line or it was nobody's, and he had learned on a white floor what it cost to hold a different ruling from the people whom he was ruling for.
chapter-59.md:69:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:He had sat in that service for fifteen years. He knew what it did with its instruments when they were done with, and he had supposed that he knew the worst of it. He had not known that it would write *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper, about a kit that was still lying where its owner knelt, and offer three hundred marks for it as a hook to hang five people on. The man with the upright hand had been a clerk of a kind. He had written true lines at somebody's bidding and had not been told what they were for.
chapter-60.md:85:\b(?:and|or|but|with|to|of|for|as|than|which|that)\s*[.!?]$:There was a third that he owed, to a friend on a bluff who lost to him every time he ran it. He had no line for that one yet. It would go by the first boat of the thaw, and he wrote in his own book that it was to.

