    67	"Nothing that I'd notice. A count of ten never does." She considered him. "You've gone the colour you went on my table. Sit down."
    68	
    69	He sat down on the turf at her feet, since his knees had some views of their own on the matter, and his right hand felt as though he had been holding it in a stream.
    70	
    71	She was silent for a little while, and behind him the grey mule was trying to eat Brom's sleeve, and being told in a low reasonable voice that it was not hay.
    72	
    73	"You'll have a minute of the long one," she said. "No more. I'll count it, and I'll say when, and you'll come out on my word and not on your own."
    74	
    75	"You said that it wasn't run on a road."
    76	
    77	"I said that I don't run it on a road, since it costs me a mending and I've none to throw about. It's you that's running this, and I'm only sitting under it." She was turning her collar down. "And a minute's no reading. It's a look through a door. But the short one shows you that there's a bed, and it never yet showed anybody what was running in it. I'd not have you go back to yourself knowing the half."
    78	
    79	She turned round on the ledge, so that her back was to him.
    80	
    81	"Your left hand on the back of my neck, under the collar. The right goes back on the wrist." She held it out behind her. "It goes along from the one and comes home to the other. You know how it goes. You've had it done."
    82	
    83	He got up on his knees. He laid his left palm on the back of her neck, under the cropped hair, where her own left hand had once lain under his for twenty minutes, and took her wrist again; and he felt her draw one long breath and let the whole of it go, as she had taught him to.
    84	
    85	"Still and whole and willing," said Oryn, to the rock in front of her. "That's the three. Go on."
    86	
    87	He let it go out, and went with it.
    88	
    89	It was the banks again at first, close and high, and he went along between them at his own uneven pace. Then he was past the place where a count of ten would have stopped him, and for the first time he was *in* a person who was not himself.
    90	
    91	There was one channel.
    92	
    93	He had not known that he expected more until he found the one. In himself there were ten things going ten ways, each running into the next like water into water; he had lain in a loft and failed to find where any of them ended. Here there was a single bed and a single thing in it, which was deep, and did not wander.
    94	
    95	And it ran.
    96	
    97	That was the difference, and it took him some of his minute to find the word for it. His own went along, quick or dragging, as he was glad or tired, and it had never seemed to him to be going anywhere in particular. Hers was going somewhere: it ran as a river runs in its last mile, when it can smell the sea. Wherever his attention rested on it, he found it already leaning that way, toward his left hand, and then toward his right; it came to meet whatever touched it. It did not pool in any place, and there was no slack water in her at all. The whole of that woman, from the neck to the heels, was on its way *to* something, and had been for so long that the banks had worn to fit.
    98	
    99	"Forty," said Oryn's voice, a long way off. "You're helping. Don't."
   100	
   101	He eased his neck. He had seen a man's shin close under those hands in an hour, and had watched her walk to a mule and unload it, for five days that she could not spare; and he had taken her for a person who gave things up. She gave nothing up. She was made as a road is made, to get to where the hurt was, and everything in her that was not road had been spent on it years ago.
   102	
   103	"Sixty. Out."
   104	
   105	He took his hands away, and the going-along came home through his right hand and lay down, and he sat back on his heels.
   106	
   107	She turned round on the ledge and put her collar up; she asked him nothing, and he understood that she did not mean to. She had sat in front of a great many people who had just been told what was in them, and she knew what it was to have the telling pulled out before it was ready.
   108	
   109	It was Cael who spoke first after all, and it was not what he had meant to say.
   110	
   111	"You're built to arrive."
   112	
   113	It was only the truest thing that he had brought back.
   114	
   115	"Yes," she said. "That's what I am."

