editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:33:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:111:"It stops short," she said. She was sitting on the top box with the leaf on her knee. "Short of what? I can think of four answers, and I've no way of choosing among them. It may stop short of the fence, or of the people behind the fence. It may be that something lies under the ground at that distance and nowhere else. Or a hundred and ten is simply as near as it ever comes to anything, and the stone is an accident."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:133:He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does, and he may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:45:Brom had found his own lesson without help. A pale trunk lay stranded at the water's edge, as thick as a man's body, and he got it up on his shoulder and began to carry it along the wet stones where the footing was worst. The rule was a simple one: when the load shifted, he was to let it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:55:He took it up again. It rolled on his shoulder at the sixth step, and Cael saw him take a decision that plainly went against the grain of his whole life, and open his hands. The trunk hit the stones and bounced, and Brom stood over it, empty-handed, with the face of a man who has let fall somebody else's child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:215:"It may be. I'll take it, though." Brom walked a little way. "I've been told what I was worth by a district panel and a provincial counter and a letter with a red seal on it. That's the first time anybody ever said it and thought he'd had the better of the bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:23:"I put that to him also. He said that the salt crews go under them twice a year on the cold road, and walk, and are let alone. The animals are as mild as cattle. A man may pass beneath a herd all day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:He let Iron Skin go as a man lets a coat fall from his shoulders in a doorway, all at once, and stood in what was underneath. The grey went out of his neck and his hands, and out of the arm with the jaws shut on it, between one beat of his heart and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:221:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:143:Cael had. Nobody could have missed it. Two-thirds of the way along the bay a blade of pale stone stood up out of the shale, quite alone, halfway between the foot of the face and the edge. It lay square across the fall of the ground, like a wall across a field, and it was as long as a barn and no thicker than a garden wall. At either end it was no higher than a man's waist, and in the middle it was twice his height, and the evening sun lay along its upper side and left the lower black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:97:"That is where the next mark would fall if the line ran straight," she said. "It won't. Watch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:297:"Quite. If water masks it, then there is one of us who may use what he has for the whole of the evening, provided that he does so up to his chin." Seln's mild face showed nothing at all. "I had meant to propose the pool beside the tail. I have not lifted my Path since a fire at a crossroads, and I find that I should rather like to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:Thirty yards off, Brom let the tail-board fall. It banged, and nobody was there to stop it. He dragged out the left-hand box and got it onto his shoulder, and the middle one after it onto the other. Then he turned with a hundredweight on either side of his head and came back over the cracking shale at a trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:189:He did not hear her land. He heard the slide going over after her, tons of it, in a long hissing fall; and after that, at intervals, three or four heavier sounds, dull and separate, that were not stone and not a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael had not known that he ever did. He walked across the yard in his slow dry way with a length of ash in his hand, peeled, as tall as his shoulder, with a fork left at the top of it for a palm to sit in, and he stood it against the trough where Lira's hand would find it. He kept his eyes off her foot; he lifted two fingers from his side, an inch, and let them fall, and went back up his ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:277:"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:"No." She let them fall. "Not the colour, and not the grain; it has no grain at all. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in this box, with a date to each, and not one family of them comes near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"If she slips with me on the inside, she goes that way with nothing to stop her. This way she's got something to lean on." Oryn clicked her tongue. "She's a mule, and she'd sooner lean than fall. Most things would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:121:The second item was the side she kept. On every slant she was below the mule. When the track turned, and the fall of the hill went over to the other hand, she passed behind the animal's tail without breaking step and came up on the low side again; and she did it at every turn of that hillside without once seeming to consider which side was the low one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:279:She let the hand fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:109:"It can't get its legs under it. Keep close to the edge. If you give it a yard of ground it'll have its spring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:145:They were at the south. Fifteen yards on along the kerb two grey shapes were standing on the grass, shoulder to shoulder, with their heads level. They had gone ahead on the white and dropped off the rim where there was nobody under it with a pole, and they stood now on turf, where a hound has all the spring that it was born with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:315:"That stays above her heart." She took Lira's left wrist and laid it against Lira's own right collarbone, and shut Lira's right hand over it. "You hold that there. You don't let it down to balance, and you don't put it out if you fall. If you fall, you fall on your face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:119:"It went for me, and I crossed it twice. Some of the stones on it are bedded and some are waiting to leave, and I could not tell the one from the other by looking; so I tried each with my weight before I trusted it, and I scratched a mark on every one that bore." He took out the narrow dull knife and showed its point, which was bright. "There are forty-four. A careful person may go over on those, facing the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:93:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:Lira was in front of him, and he watched how she went. The ankle was taped, and under a healer's order still, so that she set the great boot down whole at every stride, heel and toe together, and rolled nowhere. She had done it for fifteen days on turf. On frozen ruts it was a different trade. Twice in the first mile the boot skated, and twice her right arm went out and her left stayed where a knot held it; she had been told that she was to fall on her face sooner than put that hand out, and he saw that she had taught the hand to believe it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:103:"The man with the book wrote *on bearing*," said Seln. He had not taken the ledger out. "He drew through two, as she said in that room, and he had a third confirmed along it. Hers lie along the same three. I think that we may call it one line, and his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:They were the whole of the forenoon about that perimeter, with the marsh on their right and the frost going grey on the turf. Not once in that round did the water stand a yard inside the line, or a reed a yard beyond it. A spring had filled that basin for longer than anybody could say, and it had filled it to a line that five people could feel in their heads and no farther. Cael could not have said what water knew about such an edge, and not one of them tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:173:So he did the other thing, which anybody may do. He asked him a question, in words, that he could answer or not as he chose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:147:She took the place nearest the door. She sat on the end of the bench with her boots drawn under her, as a person sits who may have to get up, and laid her two hands on the board where she could see them. Cael watched her do it. She ate at seven gates, round after round, whatever was put before her, and went out again in the morning; and she sat at this one like a woman who has sat at a great many tables and stayed at none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:"Then you may have my bread," said Seln, "all of it, with my compliments," and sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:157:"I have that noun," she said. Her voice was very careful. "I have had it since the fifty-eighth day, from a carter's wife, in three sentences, and I have had nobody to put a question to. You have just said it to me yourself, at your own plank. I think that I may ask now. What is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:249:"So I cannot tell you how it was made, since I have nothing to compare it to. But a thing may be dated without knowing how it was made, if it has stood in the weather, and that has." She took up the cup of wands. "I wrote down which way every stone faced. I did it from habit, and it is the best thing that I did on that floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:353:"She'll fall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:355:"She may. She'll get up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:139:"That bone's a month old today. I said a month, on the mountain. It may run."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:He had that sum by heart, from the ninety-fifth day to the morning after a fortnight. Sixteen and one made seventeen, somewhere on a road that he had never seen; and she did not say where the one would fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:21:He heard it draw level, and fall in behind, and follow. It was no nearer than it had been in the reeds. A swell went with them across the last ten yards, a little to the left of Brom's heels, and Brom did not turn his head to look at it; Cael could see the back of his neck, which was red, and the care with which he was putting down each foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:29:The floor was a ledge. It went round the foot of the four walls, three or four strides broad, under a foot of water; and in the middle of the room there was no floor at all. The plates had gone in there, a long time ago. He saw a hole as wide as a cart is long, with green depth in it. The water over it stood up in a low smooth dome and slid away on every side, and that was the spring. In the very middle of the hole one great slab lay aslant, like a table that has lost two legs, with its upper corner a hand under the surface.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:Nobody moved, and for the space of ten breaths the only sounds in that place were the spring, and five people getting their wind, and the small knocking going round below them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:169:"It has heard him fall," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:239:It could not tip the slab, and it would not leave it; and so it had done what it had done at the kerb, and drawn in its length about the one thing on its floor that would not come away. But the hole was not the dish. There was no room in it. It had come up out of the green depth coil upon coil, and lay heaped round the slab and over the ledge and against the foot of three walls, with the spring pouring off its back. It was longer than the room by twice. It was the colour of mud above; and where it had rolled to get its jaws about the stone, the whole of one side lay upward, as pale as tallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:299:The spring stood up again over the place in its smooth low dome.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:185:"I have something to show," he said. "I should like it shown where the iron is. It is three cords, and she may take my arm."
