editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:199:*You were told. I shall fall more carefully.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:27:"I said that I should pace it in the morning, and the morning was otherwise engaged." She was winding the slack round her fist. "Brom may not haul; he is to stand and be seen. So I want Seln for the turfs, and I want Lira on the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:101:"No. I have six hours of oil and a roll of paper; I can sample it, and I shall have to choose." She had not taken her eyes from the stone. "It is the same hand. I may say that now, since I can see the bars."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:103:The healer had stopped at her stride and a half from the nearest wall. She had been round eleven stones on the Fallow thirty times and had never gone near enough to see that they were cut, and she had sat with her back to this for a whole day in the dark. She held up her two palms to it, slowly, as she held them to a patient whom she had not yet touched; and then she let them fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:121:"One person came to this. I have his track in from the door, a little to the left of ours, and the dust has lain on it a long while. He knelt here, and here, and I cannot find where he went out." He glanced up. "That may be the dust; I say only that I can read his coming and I cannot read his going. There has been nobody since, and nothing is tied to it, and nothing is under it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:115:She was as wet down the front as a person can be who has lain for an hour on her stomach on a stone with a spring slopping over it. Cael saw her shiver, from the near fire, and saw that she did not know she was doing it; she had her notebook open and three clean leaves before her, and her pencil had already begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:He had stood on a wall that morning and looked down at a spring. The water there had come up against a fallen slab and piled, and curled back behind it in a slow eddy, as water does when it meets a thing lying in its road. He looked for the piling now, and there was none. Not one of the ten came up against that place. Each of them began to lean a long way short of it, and came back to its line a long way past; they went by it as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river, without a ripple, along a way that was worn smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had halted on a level of short turf where a spring came out. Brom had been helped off with the box and lay on his back beside it with his arms spread. The others had gone to the water. Lira had not; she had sat down next to Cael, and stretched out both legs, and was looking at Hesk's book where it lay on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:93:"I shall not be a reason. You shut that thing for my sake, and you have kept it shut at a cost that I watched you pay in a river. But I have had four nights in which to consider who was looking in a lane, and I find that I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant. Strike the second. It is not available to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:101:"That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:She took up the cheek-strap, and let it fall again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:131:"It is an observation. You may put my initial against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:167:"I know where you had it. I said it to a loft, with my hand on a ladder." She rubbed her palms together slowly. "It's what I say at every gate when I leave it. I don't ask them whether I may go. I tell them where I'll be, and then it's theirs to carry, and they can't say afterward that they weren't told. I never had it said to me before. I don't care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:"It's that. The door's shut, and I'm on the wrong side of it from where I keep the thing." He let his arms fall. "It's queer. I've not used it since the ford, and I'd have told you I'd got used to going without. But I always knew where it was. I could feel it in the next room, as you can feel a fire through a wall. There's no next room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:11:"May I read you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:105:She had not spoken to him since the yard. She had sat at a board with him, and had eaten a goat; it was not the same thing, and he did not make the mistake of taking it for one. A person may sit at a table with somebody whom she has not let off, as she may mend a man whom she does not like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:233:"I have a name for it. I want it said to four people before it goes into the box, as he says a line under his eave. Then any of you may call it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:27:It went from under her on the fourth day, on a sheet of ice where a spring had crossed the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:93:To the south there were two shapes still. One of them, the one that had stood all day with its arms folded, raised a single arm above its hood and let it fall. Then both stooped, and straightened, and went away over the rim to the south, with nothing in their hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:"I have thought about that too," said Seln. "I could walk up those steps now, with this held over my head. They would take it. I do not think that the fence would come down afterward. A paper that sends six people to a hole in the ground is not concerned with what becomes of anybody who could say how the thing was collected." He paused. "I may be wrong. I have been inside that service, and I do not think that I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:"After, he is to do everything that he has forbidden himself all day, as loudly as he can, on the far side of that stone. Their whole plan is that nobody on this ground has a Path. The instant that one of us stands outside the fence with something in his hands, and the fence is down behind him, there is no plan. Five more of us may walk out and be what we are." Seln's head turned toward Cael. "They were sent for a case. They were not sent to fight in the dark for it. I know what my own orders would have been, and so do they."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see. He had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:141:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:293:He carried eleven sacks more before the light went, on feet that told him of each rung a little after he had climbed it, and he did not fall off the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:299:"Nobody," said Seln. "I walked the rise before we came down, and there is no rim to this place; you can see a mile of wet grass from here on every side. He may do as he thinks fit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:97:She shut the door and let the peg fall. She did not take the stool. She carried the book to the sill, into the square of light, and laid it open on the strip of canvas among her own things, between the shears and the horn cup; and Cael thought that he had never seen an odder row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:235:She had eaten, and had her two hours on her back, with the door shut and the book on the sill. She came out and washed to the elbow in the bucket, and she washed more slowly than she ever did, as a carter walks who thinks that his axle may be cracked and would sooner not hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:"There's the store behind the chimney," he said. "It's warm; it has the back of that fire for a wall. I keep meal in it." He looked at the three boxes, which Brom and Seln had brought in out of the snow. "Six, for the cold term. And you may lie at this end of the hearth for the standing-money, the five of you, as long as there's paper in my store."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.

