chapter-32.md:27: Nine had come home across that floor in no order, and one of them had sat down to scratch. Two had walked onto Brom's pole from the same place, a minute apart, with the second watching the first. Eight had stood by while a bow was loosed among them. They could have cut this lane ahead of the file at any stride of it, and they had not troubled to; they kept their station on the slowest thing that was walking, and waited for it to stop.
chapter-35.md:221: It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
chapter-36.md:187: "I'm going to do it," he said. "I've heard every one of you, and you're right. The right thing is to cross that yard tonight; or, failing that, to tell her in the morning before she has laid a hand on me. I know that. I am going to lie down on that table and say nothing, and tell her the minute that it's over. I want you to know that I chose it with all of this in front of me. Nobody talked me into it, and nobody was kept from talking me out."
chapter-37.md:1: # Chapter 37 — Minute Twenty-One
chapter-37.md:5: A man asleep draws twelve to the minute. He had timed Brom against Hesk's clock once, on a road, for want of anything better to do, and had kept the figure. Twelve to the minute made sixty in five, and two hundred and forty in the twenty that she had named; and he lay on a scrubbed plank with a healer's hand over his breastbone and began at one.
chapter-37.md:95: It was in the thirteenth minute that he understood what was strange about her.
chapter-37.md:113: She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was no weaker, but it had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge: under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
chapter-37.md:131: He was at two hundred and twenty-eight. That left one minute of her twenty.
chapter-37.md:163: So he knew which was right, and he chose against it. He was exact about why. It was not that a leaf permitted him; a thing written down beforehand is only a thing done on purpose. It was that he wanted to know what he was more than he wanted to be in the right, and the one instrument that might tell him was standing at his breastbone. He would answer for it within the minute, on this table, to her face.
chapter-38.md:87: "That it was wrong." Brom's eyes stayed on her and kept away from the plank. "Each of us, one after another, in our own words. He said we were right. He said the right thing was to come across this yard and knock, or to tell you this morning before you'd washed your hands, and that he would do neither, and that he'd tell you the minute it was over."
chapter-38.md:115: "And your four told you. I've sat with that a minute, and I find it's the part I mind most. It wasn't a thing you did in the dark, by yourself, without anybody to say what it was. You'd been told its name by four people, and you'd agreed with them."
chapter-38.md:181: "Good." Oryn gave him the book. "Now lie down again. Not for me. You're the colour of the wall and you've been standing on that calf for a quarter of an hour, and I've just been told I'm a patient's source, and I'd like a minute to sit with the second thing before I go and eat."
chapter-38.md:185: She had her minute. He gave it to her with his eyes on the rafters, and nothing in that room moved but the light on the sill.
chapter-39.md:117: He lay down, and was asleep inside a minute, as he was anywhere.
chapter-40.md:257: Seln passed it, and she read it for herself, once, and gave it on to Karis. Cael had written a healer down as a stop, in pencil, a minute before a one-armed man's line came in at the door to say that the country had already entered her otherwise. A clerk's fair copy of that same count was in a boat by now, going west to whoever had put the question.
chapter-48.md:73: "You'll have a minute of the long one," she said. "No more. I'll count it, and I'll say when, and you'll come out on my word and not on your own."
chapter-48.md:77: "I said that I don't run it on a road, since it costs me a mending and I've none to throw about. It's you that's running this, and I'm only sitting under it." She was turning her collar down. "And a minute's no reading. It's a look through a door. But the short one shows you that there's a bed, and it never yet showed anybody what was running in it. I'd not have you go back to yourself knowing the half."
chapter-48.md:97: That was the difference, and it took him some of his minute to find the word for it. His own went along, quick or dragging, as he was glad or tired, and it had never seemed to him to be going anywhere in particular. Hers was going somewhere: it ran as a river runs in its last mile, when it can smell the sea. Wherever his attention rested on it, he found it already leaning that way, toward his left hand, and then toward his right; it came to meet whatever touched it. It did not pool in any place, and there was no slack water in her at all. The whole of that woman, from the neck to the heels, was on its way *to* something, and had been for so long that the banks had worn to fit.
chapter-48.md:277: *Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
chapter-53.md:339: "In a minute."
chapter-53.md:341: "Then have your minute. Nobody else is to stop when we go."
chapter-55.md:177: He knew now what he was looking for. He had been in a river with banks, on a ridge, for a minute, and had felt them stand close and high on either hand like the walls of a mill-race. He went the whole length of the Wind and found none. The millstone was under it, and ran into it, and he could not have said where; he tried the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, which was thinner tonight than he had ever heard it, and they were ten, and not one of them had an edge.
chapter-55.md:227: "You're going to tell me about a thing that I'll never get my hands on. I'd take a finding from a second healer across a table, if I had felt the patient. I've not felt this one. I've had a river in the dark off you seven times, and that's all that I'll ever have." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "But you were in me for a minute, on that ridge, and I felt you go along. I know your step. If you're in me while you say it, I'll have the thing that found it, under my own skin, while it tells me what it found. It's the nearest that I'll come."
chapter-55.md:237: "Yes. Hand on the wrist. The short reading first, to ten, and then the neck, and the long reading for one minute, which I'll count. You'll talk while you're in. If I say *out*, you come out with the sentence in your mouth."
chapter-56.md:165: The tenth did not stir, since it never asked for anything. But he knew what was within his reach, as a man with a key in his pocket knows a lock when he passes one. It would take two fingers, and a count of ten. At three he would have this man's banks under his hand, and at ten he would know the Path as he knew a friend's step. In a minute more he would have what ran in it, and how deep. That was the Bronze which a one-armed man had guessed at under his eave, and had never been told. A registrar would have given a year's pay for what lay a foot from his fingers. He could have had the whole of it and told nobody.
