chapter-34.md:61: The big man came down out of the north when the east had begun to grey, walking as a person walks who has stood since midnight, with a bow in his two hands and a rag round one of them.
chapter-35.md:41: It was a strange afternoon. They spoke of a good many things. Lira asked how far a mule could smell a dead dog, and the healer told her. Brom wished to know whether a bow was meant to pull to the left or whether that was him. Karis wrote that she would open the box under a roof and not before, since there was wind, and held it up; and they agreed that there was wind.
chapter-35.md:287: He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
chapter-39.md:59: She gave him the leather sleeve without a word. He counted what was in it, touching each iron head with his thumb: there were five. Then he took down the bow from the peg where it had hung since the ninety-ninth evening and looked along it, as he would have looked along an axle.
chapter-39.md:61: "There's a hook for this in her near pannier. I'll ask her for it when she's on her feet, and not before; she's had enough taken off her this morning." He hung the bow on his shoulder. "And the carter went down light at first light, with Karis's four lines in his hat for the one-armed man, and the hold's mark under them. So that's gone, and it says six."
chapter-39.md:243: On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
chapter-41.md:115: "It isn't too many. It's hide by now." She let the hand go. "You've the hook on your belt. Keep it there. That bow's no good to anybody with its hook in my pannier, and I ought to have known it at noon on that hill."
chapter-42.md:119: "I know who I've got on that road a good deal better than you do." She said it without heat. "And I'll not have spent a night of myself on an arm and then walked off from it in the week that it wanted watching. Those are my five days. You didn't ask for them, and you're not to carry them. Go and shoot your bow."
chapter-42.md:133: It was a bow, and it was not his kind. His own leaned against the fence, as tall as he was. This was short and thick, an ash stock as long as her arm with a bow of horn and wood lashed across the nose of it, and an iron stirrup, and a long lever underneath. It was very old. Somebody had kept it oiled.
chapter-42.md:149: "A bow wants one hand," said the bowman, "and a thing that doesn't move."
chapter-42.md:189: She sat down on the planks with her back against a post. He did not explain. He stood with his own long bow at the length of his arm and looked down the valley; and by and by she understood that he meant to stand there until she was finished, however long that took.
chapter-42.md:283: Lira came up the ladder at noon and found the bow lying on the planks in its goatskin, and the lean man standing in the worn place himself, with his own left hand in it.
chapter-42.md:323: "Why did you come down?" she said. "With the bow. I never asked you."
chapter-42.md:339: *What am I becoming, if nobody's measuring.* She had asked that at a fire, and nobody had been able to tell her. She did not know now. But there had never been such a thing before as a Wind who could not run, and might not catch, and shot another person's bow off a rail with one arm; and she thought that she would like to see what that was by the time there was snow.
chapter-43.md:221: She shot from the rail first. Cael could see only the nose of the bow over the fence, and one goose feather; he heard her breath go out, and then nothing at all, for so long that Brom shifted his feet and one of the carters began to say something and was hushed. Then there was the slap.
chapter-43.md:233: There was a scuffling on the planks, and the nose of the bow went down out of sight.
chapter-43.md:251: On the gate-walk a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of his own bow, an inch, and laid them back.
chapter-43.md:253: Brom had the healer's bow on his shoulder. He looked from it to the fence.
chapter-43.md:267: She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
chapter-44.md:33: "A light," said Brom. "Going up that hill the wrong way, with a noise on the end of it like a glass. And every dog on the slope turned its back on us and went after it. I'd a bow up and nothing whatever to shoot."
chapter-45.md:11: It was the hardest cold that they had yet walked in. The ruts of the valley road had frozen as the last grain cart left them, with every hoof-mark standing up like a thing cut in iron, and the pale grass on either hand was white to the knee. Their breath went ahead of them. On the gate-walk a lean man stood with his long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every morning that they had spent behind that fence; and when Lira looked up, he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had nothing in her right hand, and she lifted two of her own.
chapter-48.md:249: "We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
chapter-48.md:285: They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
chapter-49.md:17: Brom came last. Cael heard him grunt at the stride, as he had grunted on the Fallow, like a man who has felt a door shut behind him. The healer's bow was on his left shoulder, spanned, with a bolt lying in its groove.
chapter-49.md:41: Something was wrong with that shot, and though he had only fifteen yards to cover, it took him the whole of them to say what. A bow on that brow could have let all five of them walk down to the floor, and had them two hundred yards inside, with nothing over them and nowhere to go. It had chosen to shoot the last man instead, when he was five strides from the way out, with a healer standing in plain sight at the door.
chapter-49.md:91: There were two on the southern rim, a quarter of the way round. They had risen out of the white grass behind the ruts while he was looking at the brow. One of them was kneeling over a bow, and the other only stood, a little apart, with his arms folded.
chapter-49.md:127: She did not wait to be answered. She stepped in close under Brom's good arm, and with her right hand she lifted the healer's bow off his left shoulder by its stock, spanned as it was, with the bolt held in the groove under her thumb.
chapter-49.md:177: "Keep on," she said, between her teeth. "I've got it. I've got the bow. Keep *on*."
chapter-49.md:221: "Why not?" said Lira. She was sitting on the lowest step with the bow across her knees and her left side turned away from the light.
chapter-49.md:225: Lira looked at the bow.
chapter-49.md:289: "It was the twentieth step," she said. She was looking at the wall above the healer's head. "The ankle went. I'd the bow in my right hand, so I'd nothing to put out. And then it was out. I never told it to. It hit the wall, and it took me, the whole of me, and I stood up off it."
chapter-50.md:7: Lira sat on the thirty-fourth step, six above the floor, with the bow along her knee, and Seln stood below her in the doorway at the stair's foot. Brom was on the ground against the wall to the north of that doorway, with three coats on him, and the healer sat on her heels at his left hand. Karis had put her back against the wall opposite; and in the middle of the floor, where nobody went, a case lay in the dark with its cord.
chapter-50.md:17: "The one at the gate with the bow is doing what you would do. He is finding his range."
chapter-50.md:51: Cael had gone to the foot of the stair, where he could see her against the white, a dark shape with one arm, and the bow lying along her knee with its nose toward the square. It had not stirred.
chapter-50.md:55: "At what? There's nobody in it." She did not turn her head. "I've one in the groove. If I loose it at a patch of sky I've an empty bow that I can't span, and Seln has to come up six steps into the light to do it for me. He'd like that, up there."
chapter-50.md:59: "I've stopped. I'll go on being stopped till there's something standing in that square." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "It stays in the bow."
chapter-50.md:125: Cael got up from the wall, and his legs were stiff. He went across to the foot of the stair, to be nearer the light, and found that Lira had turned on her step to meet him, six above the floor, and was sitting with her chin on the stock of the bow.
chapter-51.md:11: The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
chapter-51.md:17: The bow came round.
chapter-51.md:31: *One.* Everything that he owned went dumb. For the length of that count there was a boy standing on a hillside who could not have shifted a foot to save his life; and he spent it looking up at the brow, since his eyes were the only part of him that would answer. The man with the bow had not stood to watch his bolt. He had the nose of the thing on the turf already, and his boot going into the stirrup.
chapter-51.md:49: He had run up a ditch on the Fallow in that lag, with hounds behind him, and he knew it; but he had not known what it was to close with a man in it. His boots struck the turf, and then he was told that they had struck. The brow came up to meet him. There was a hooded shape in a cloud of chaff with one arm across its face, and it had let the string go half drawn and caught the bow up by its stirrup end.
chapter-51.md:59: The hooded man had both his hands stung off the bow. It fell between them.
chapter-51.md:85: It was the bowman who ended it. He had got the chaff out of his cloth, and he bent, without haste, and took up the bow from the turf. He did something to the lock of it with his thumb, and a small dark thing like a walnut came away into his palm and went into his coat. He drew a short knife across the string, and then he laid the bow down again in the ruts, carefully, on its side, as a workman lays down a tool that was only lent him.
chapter-51.md:179: He brought it into the lamplight and turned it over, slowly, as he turned over any paper. It was a good bow, heavier than the healer's, with a prod of horn and sinew, and its string hung cut from one end. He ran his thumb along the underside of the stock, and under the stirrup, and across the butt.
chapter-51.md:181: "There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
chapter-51.md:227: It was a lantern, and it was carried high, at the length of somebody's arm, as a person carries one who wishes it understood from a long way off that he is not creeping. Seln was on his feet before Cael had seen it. Lira had the bow across her knee.
chapter-51.md:281: But he could be half a day from where a certain paper pointed, with a spear and a bow and a boy, on honest business. Nobody could say that it rested on nothing, since it rested on three stakes and four marks.
chapter-51.md:283: They walked at noon, and slept at the fork above the mill, without a fire, a crew's length apart; and before he lay down he counted his three by their breathing, as he did every night of his life. The bow's was the short one, since that hip had not liked the ridge.
chapter-51.md:297: "Look for what?" said the bow. He was easing his leg out along the turf, a little at a time.
chapter-51.md:305: The spear said nothing, which was what he said to most things. The bow grunted, and went on easing his leg. The boy put the bread into his mouth.
chapter-51.md:311: He set two stakes with the spear and the boy, in water to the thigh, while the bow sat on the bank and sharpened the third. He did the sum as he drove them: it was an afternoon up to that place, by the ruts, and a morning to go in and bring a case out, if it was a walk and a carry, so that they would be down at the ford again by the middle of the day.
chapter-51.md:325: Not one of the three asked him why. The spear took his spear. The bow got up off the bank, on the good leg first, and slung his stave; and Teague, who had been about to tell him that he might stay with the mill and the mules, saw his face and held his tongue.
chapter-51.md:351: That was a crossbow. He had expected a bow of some kind. He had not expected that every sign of its owner would stop at the height of a boot.
chapter-51.md:363: The bow had the three of them across his knee before they were fairly on the grass. He turned each to the lantern, and tried each empty lock with his thumb, and shook his head over them like a man at a horse fair.
chapter-51.md:381: He put the spear on the southern rim, where two had stood, and sent the boy west, since the boy had the legs for it. The bow he set on the north, which was the shortest walk, and he took the brow himself.
chapter-52.md:345: Brom had taken up one of the shafts, and he tried it along the stock of the healer's bow, where it stood proud of its channel by the thickness of a finger, and would not lie down.
chapter-53.md:353: "There's a way to do it," said Seln. "It has given us its rule, and a rule is a plan. On the floor, we move. We rest only on what comes up out of the old work: the path, and the kerb, and the tops of those walls, which are a yard through. A wall-top is a kerb set on end." He counted it off. "We go in at first light. Lira carries the bow, spanned, since she can loose it and cannot bend it. I carry the hook and the sleeve. Karis has her paper and nothing else. Thirty yards of plates, at a walk, with nobody standing; in at the gap; and up."
chapter-53.md:371: Lira had the healer's bow across her knee, and she was looking past the fire at the dark place where the reeds began.
chapter-54.md:5: Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
chapter-54.md:31: "Left," said Seln. "The low place. I go up, and I take the bow from her, and then her hand."
chapter-54.md:33: He went at the first of the breaks without slackening, and put both palms on the round of it, and was sitting on the wall's top before Cael had seen how. Lira held the bow up to him by its stock. He laid it behind him, and took her right hand in both of his, and she walked up the cut face of the wall on her whole sole as though it had been a stair.
chapter-54.md:61: He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
chapter-54.md:79: "Karis," said Lira. "Here; I've the bow. Cael's along from us, at the corner. You're across the hole. Brom?"
chapter-54.md:85: Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
chapter-54.md:119: "No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
chapter-54.md:177: Lira had the bow up, and nothing to loose it at but water.
chapter-54.md:253: She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
chapter-54.md:257: Her left arm lay where a healer had tied it, high against the collar-bone, and did not stir. A lean man had told her on a gate-walk that a bow wants one hand, and a thing that does not move.
chapter-54.md:269: Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
chapter-54.md:279: Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
chapter-54.md:303: "It is alive," said Seln. He was coming back along the wall with the bow. "And it is going down, and it is hurt, and I do not know how long any of those will last. Karis. Paper. We walk."
chapter-54.md:315: On the kerb, which was the hall's outer coping, Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.
chapter-55.md:23: Lira held nothing; she sat on the grey stone with the bow across her knee, and looked from the healer's hands to the reeds, and back.
chapter-55.md:121: Lira had not left the grey stone. She sat on it with her foot set down whole and the healer's bow along her knee, with its nose toward the reeds.
chapter-55.md:125: "Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
chapter-55.md:131: He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
chapter-55.md:155: *Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
chapter-56.md:89: "For them," said the bow.
chapter-56.md:105: She had put her spoon down. He knew her opinion, since she had given it to him over the bow's hip more times than he could count. A thing put right in an hour by a Path was a thing that nobody afterward thought to tie. Hands were quick, she said, and quick was not the same as held.
chapter-56.md:123: "Well?" the bow had said, when he came in.
chapter-56.md:135: There was a camp at the track's end with two fires. He counted his own first: a spear, and a bow, and a boy, and an old woman with a board. Then he counted the camp, and there were six in it.
chapter-57.md:159: He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
chapter-58.md:23: Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
chapter-58.md:59: There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
chapter-58.md:85: Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
chapter-58.md:101: By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
chapter-59.md:29: He was three strides behind. He saw the boot go, and her right arm fly out; and he saw the left, under the grey wool, start to come and stop at a knot. She went down on her right shoulder and her hip, hard, with the bow clattering on her back. She lay for one breath with her cheek on the ice.
chapter-60.md:141: She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
