editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:169:"Nobody leaves a registry kit. It's signed out. You don't get another." Seln's mild voice had an edge in it that Cael had heard only once before, by a grey stone, the night before. "I signed for a satchel and four keys in my first posting, and I answered for every one of them for fifteen years. A surveyor answers for this. If he had walked out of that door on his own feet, he would have carried it, or he would have come back for it. And the people who sent us here knew that it was lying. They wrote *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper. They left it where it was for as long as they wanted a thing in this country that somebody could be sent to fetch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: fourteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:163:*Three. Deployment since the Line: twenty-six.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:301:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:215:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:61:*HOLD.* It was in the square capitals, and it was not his. There were five names under it, and five again under the same word on the shingle beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:219:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:*Filed with your record. It reads: left in good standing, undefeated on paper. I've kept this ledger forty years and never had a line like it. The session still stands. The road still runs through Ardenmere. — V.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:145:"They say a healer's no good there. That's the words. *You'll not want her in the ring; she's no good there.* I had it first from a carter at the first gate I ever mended at, and I took it that he meant I'd be frightened." Oryn laced the boot. "Then I had it from six more, in the same words, and not one of them a man to be frightened of a field. So it's a thing that was seen once, by somebody, and handed down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:63:*Hundred and twenty-second day, the fork. Oryn north. On her terms. I didn't ask. Five on the bearing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the inventory beneath, since a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried. There were ten confirmed. He set down the nine in their order, each with the Path that it stood beside and the person it had come from, and against the second of them he wrote *the Bronze from Ardenmere*, as he always did. He set down the tenth: Tide-adjacent, from Oryn; partial; Iron; himself, and whatever his hand was on. It was the first that did nothing in a fight, and the first that he had reached for knowing what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:377:*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:7:"*Cael,*" said Pike. He was frowning at a sheet folded very small. "And under it, *past the Line.* I've had that direction once before." He looked over the heads, twice. "I've the one Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:73:*— E.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:Cael had read the last lines but two again, to himself. *I still lose to you every time I run it.* He could see the bout as it had gone, on sixty feet of sprung oak, with a blade's edge declared against him in earnest and nothing held back on either side; and he could see a tall, exact person walking it through afterward in an empty yard, alone, looking for the place where it turned. Something in Cael stirred at the thought, quick and bright, and asked *Edge?* of the nearest arris, which was the lip of the wash. He told it no. It was Ephram's, that question. He carried it for him, unspent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:It was the only thing that was said about it. Cael heard the pencil going, steadily, on thin paper against stone. He heard Karis say *sheet*, in a voice that had the blood in it, and Lira let go of the belt, and tore one from the roll with her teeth, and passed it down, and took hold again. A finished sheet came up over the edge and was pinned under a pebble. The two of them moved a yard along the wall on their knees, and Karis went over once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:123:It went on for the better part of an hour. The mist thinned over their heads and went from grey to white. Seln stood on the tall wall and said *there* each time that the line of lifting plates passed below him, and it passed a great deal oftener than once in a quarter of an hour; the thing had given up its round, and was going about that one room as a dog goes about a tree. Cael's breeches froze to the stone. He counted the sheets as they came up, and then he made himself stop, since a man who is counting paper is not watching a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:67:*Nineteen. I'm told it's late; the ferry's fault, not mine. Your grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. This one's true to a hair. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:79:*Nineteen, past the Line, and there's a roof. It's an inn. The shape you said to mind is a line, and I've got the tool for it now. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:"That one I have walked. I have its round, and its radius, and eleven sheets." The finger moved a long way west and south, to a blank among the carter's hill-hatching. "That is a day south of the ferry and a little aside; I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and wrote *guess*." It moved again, far down toward the corner of the hide, where the strokes of his water lay thick. "That is a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and it is a guess as well. And the fourth is not on this skin at all." She lifted her eyes to the loft door. "It is on a sheet that he counted once, as a boy, and cannot place. One sheet had two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:249:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:65:Seln knew that counter. He had stood at one like it in his first posting, and had been given a satchel and four keys and a sheet to put his name to. The storeman had not looked up. *Returned or accounted for,* the sheet said, above the place for the name. He had thought nothing of it in his first year. It had not come into his head that there was a third thing which could happen to a man and his issue, and that the sheet had no line for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:He had sat in that service for fifteen years. He knew what it did with its instruments when they were done with, and he had supposed that he knew the worst of it. He had not known that it would write *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper, about a kit that was still lying where its owner knelt, and offer three hundred marks for it as a hook to hang five people on. The man with the upright hand had been a clerk of a kind. He had written true lines at somebody's bidding and had not been told what they were for.

