editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:It was all either of them said about it. There was a fence on the oak, four slips along: the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD. Teague drew its tack and held it out, and Pike wrote his name to it before he shut the book and tucked it under what was left of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-101-Teague stood a breath longer in front of two slips with five names on each.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-215-*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-216-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-219-*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-221----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:223:He gave the paper back to Pike in the morning, across the oak, before the mist was off the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:225:Pike took it without a word. He read it twice, though he could have said it in his sleep, and then he turned his shoulder to the meadow and gave it out: the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the rise, and the hundred. It was the first time in a month that those words had been heard on that grass. He said the whole of it a second time. Then he said *taken*, and five names, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-227-Cael counted who heard it, since a thing said at that plank was only as good as its hearers.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-231-The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:The eleventh was at Pike's elbow. He was a thin young man with ink on his cuffs and a ledger under his arm, and he had come up from the landing at a trot when he was sent for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:235:"You're the witness for the hirer," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-237-"I am told so." The clerk looked at the five as if they had been bales of a kind that he had not handled before. "My master had it by the last sack, sealed, with the sum counted in a bag and a line of direction. He was to post it and to lodge the price. Whatever is brought down, I am to see it at the landing and put our mark to the slip, and it goes west by the next boat." He swallowed. "I don't know what the things are. I was not told who wants them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:239:"Nobody's asked you," said Pike, and wrote five short strokes at the foot of the cream paper, side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-241-"What if they're not there?" said Lira. "The things. Somebody else might have picked them up. It's been lying under your stone a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:243:"Then you come down and tell me so, and I say that." Pike was blowing on the strokes to dry them. "I write what was done. There's a hundred in my box against his mark on this paper, and if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut. I've nothing in the book that pays a crew for walking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-244-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-245-"So we could go four days and come home with a line and no money."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-251-There was one thing more, and Cael had turned it over in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-252-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:253:"The two holds," he said to Pike. "With our names. We'll be nine days on this, if it goes as it's written. If we're not down in their fortnight, don't keep a bull waiting on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:255:"I'd not have. I told them a fortnight, and after that the top of the sheet." Pike blotted the strokes. "It's good of you to say it where he can hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-257-He had not said it for that. But he saw, from the corner of his eye, that the boy with the bread had stopped chewing, and was looking at his captain.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md-291-Brom carried her the four strides, on one arm, with the bow in the other hand, and Oryn was on her knees in the grass before Lira's back had touched it. Both her hands went round the arm, above the place and below it, as they had gone round a shin on a mountain, and her eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md-292-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:293:"Nothing," Oryn said, to the arm. Her hands were to the wrist in what was coming out of it. "Nothing in my hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md-294-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md-295-It was Karis who moved first. She had been bound in a barn once, by a woman with two lanterns, and had watched what that woman did before anything else; and she had both her small hands shut round Lira's arm above the elbow, on the inside, before the healer's had come away.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-39-It was Lira who said it, from the cart's tail, loudly and at once, before anybody could be sorry for her. She had called her own faults in a yard in that voice, to be ahead of the coach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:41:"There's not. They stop inside, where the tiers are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-43-The woman said it as she might have said that barley stopped at a certain height on a hill. It was a thing about the ground, and nobody had decided it. One healer walked a route, and was not due; the rest of that trade kept to the country where somebody ranked it.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-229-Eleven days ago he could hardly see the oak for paper. The dry roads had done the reverse of what they had done before; crews had gone out by all five roads and taken their slips with them, and the meadow was down to nine tents and a few carts under the ash. The tarred wood showed its tack-holes again in drifts, and by the right-hand post there was a clean place the size of a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:231:It was the middle of the morning on the sixtieth day. They had crossed the wash at first light, with Brom walking wide of the cart and both hands behind his back, and they had come over the saddle slowly. Pike was not at his plank, and the book was not out; a boy at the nearest fire said that he was down at the landing, since the ferry had brought a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:233:So the five of them stood under the long eave, and waited to be paid. Nobody in the meadow came near. But a woman frying dough had stopped with her fork in the air when they came by, and she had not yet gone back to her pan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-235-Seln was reading the board.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-279----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:281:Pike came up from the landing with a sack over his shoulder and the book under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-282-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-283-He saw them from thirty yards off. He looked at a sling, and a stick, and two small hands in strips of linen, and at how the fifth of them stood with his weight on one leg; and then he looked at all of it again, and did not quicken his walk.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-291-He set the book down, and put the stone on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-292-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:293:Brom had gone to the plank's far end. He stood there by himself, with the sewn arm in its sling, and Cael, who knew him, could see that he had been getting ready for this since the saddle and perhaps for a good deal longer. He waited until Pike had come along the oak to the ink-pot. Then he said it, low, and he did not make a question of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-294-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-295-"What does the board pay a man with no Path at all."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-297-Lira turned her head, and Karis had gone still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-298-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:299:Pike kept his eyes on the sack. He had its neck between his teeth and was working at the knot with his one hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-300-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-301-"Same as anyone," he said.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-313-"He'd put it down a breath before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-314-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:315:Pike looked at him then; he took in the big plain troubled face and the arm in its sling, and he went over them a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-316-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-317-"Then that's a fifth of forty," he said. "Eight marks. I'd pay eight for that to a Gold or to a goat, if the goat came back." He dipped his pen. "There's no column for what it was done with. I thought you'd read that off the page the first day."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-325-"Can I have one of those?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-326-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:327:Pike pulled off the nearest. It was a scrap of hide that had once wanted the stones picked out of a field, and its back was clean; he pushed it along the oak, and after a moment he pushed the pen after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-328-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-329-Brom wrote with his right hand, slowly, and his tongue showed at the corner of his mouth. He reached across and dipped in the horn pot that stood in its hole, and Cael had never seen any hand go into that hole but one.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-333-It was the second thing anybody had ever said to him that he meant to keep. The first had been four words from a coach at a tail-board, on the morning they left the hill, and he had only needed to remember those.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-334-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:335:"That's a dead slip," said Pike. "It's no use to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-336-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-337-"I know what it is."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-339-Brom nodded to him, as one tradesman to another, and walked off down the meadow toward the ash, like a man who has set down something heavy and has not yet decided whether to miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-340-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:341:Pike watched his back, as he had watched it once before, on the day he paid out two worn coins.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-342-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md-343-"Held a dog with a shoulder and a river, that one."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-3-They were out of Lowmarch two hours before the light on the hundred and thirteenth day, with the stars still hard over the river and every blade in the meadow standing white.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:It was Seln's hour, and Cael had given it to him without argument. A loaded cart took the whole of a short day to reach the mill; a crew that walked as they walked could be on the ridge by sunrise and at the fork before noon, and Seln wished to have the afternoon in hand. So Brom loaded the mule by feel. Lira ate standing. They went past Pike's bare plank and the smith's cold chimney, and took the south road in file, with the frost ringing under five pairs of boots and four small hooves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:They passed the well at the fork in the dark. It had a new rope on its windlass, and somebody had mended the curb where six stones had been set; and Cael, who had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, put his hand on the stonework as he went by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-9-The road climbed from there. By the time there was grey enough to see by they were on the back of a long ridge, bare and broad, with pale grass on it that the wind had combed all one way. The country fell off on both hands. To the east it was hills beyond hills, hatched with the red-barked trees that Karis would not name. To the west the ground went down in long folds toward a valley that he could not yet see into, and a line of mist lay along the bottom of it like wool caught on a fence.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-17-One pair kept the ridge. The other turned down the western face, slantwise, through a notch that wheels had worn, and went out of sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:19:"The fork," said Seln. He had walked the first hours of that road by himself, in their second week at Lowmarch, and had never said what he found. "The left hand is ours, later. The right is the mill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-21-They heard the wheel before they saw a roof.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-25-It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:27:A woman was sitting on the nearer of the flat stones with a net of some kind across her knees. She raised her head at the sound of hooves, and counted them down the track with her chin, one at a time, as Pike counted a meadow. Then she turned and called toward the mill, without getting up, in a voice that had been pitched to carry over a wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-29-"Five off the ridge."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-125-Karis had been reading it upside down, from his other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:127:"She looked at your hand before she said it," Karis murmured. Her voice was pitched for his ear alone. "And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-129-"It's not mine to enter."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-145-Cael told her, since it was his to tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:He gave it in order and left nothing out: a line that was said twice at a hearth, and a nail, and a sheet of cream paper that had lain sealed in a broker's box beside the first. He gave her the sum on it. He told her what Seln had found in its margin, and what the five of them had made of it at a fire, and that one of the five had said walk away and been entered. He said that the place was half a day up the ridge from the fork over her head, and that he meant to be at its edge by dusk and across it in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-149-"You said not without you. So I've not come to ask you for anything. I've come so that you should have it from me today, with the bench done, and not from a carter in a fortnight."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-241-They forded below the mill, and the woman on the flat stone counted six of them and eight hooves across without lifting her net; and they climbed out of the green valley into the wind again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:At the fork there was a flat rock, and Cael sat down on it with Hesk's book while the two mules were let to blow. The grey had come up the notch backward for a good part of the way. The mill's wheel was still to be heard below them, very small. He wrote under what he had written on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-244-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-247-He read it over. Then he looked at six people on a bare ridge, and at the left-hand ruts, which ran on to the south with the white sun over them.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-93-They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:95:The plank under the lower inn's eave was bare of its book. Pike had taken it in for the night, as he did when the dusk came early; but a line of yellow showed under the inn's door, and the noise of the room came through the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-97-"He's not said ours," said Brom. "The Fallow. He wrote it eleven days ago, and sent it up to us on a piece of bark, and it's not been said where we could hear it."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-103----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:105:Pike said it before the door was shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-107-He was on the hearth bench, under the sailcloth, with the ledger open beside him and the horn pot at his elbow; and his eyes came up as the cold came in. His look went over the five in the doorway once, and then a second time, as it went over everything. Then he turned his shoulder to the room and gave it out, in the voice that he used under his eave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-111-Then he said the whole of it again.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-131-Cael had seen that man count three on a sill before he looked at a room. He had been told by him on a river bank what a mountain was worth. He had never once seen him stand up for anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:Pike had dipped his pen. He did not write with it. The line had been in his book for eleven days. He raised his eyes to the sailcloth over his head, and found the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-135-"Eleventh. Teague's ten up."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-149-"It has."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:151:Pike was looking up at her from under his own handwriting, and he did not seem displeased to be asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-153-"You've read my lines. Every one says what came back, and what was paid on it. That one says the things weren't there and the box stayed shut. A hold comes down to my plank with a job to give, and asks me who fetches what he's sent for. I've a crew that was sent for a thing and came home without it." He held up his one hand. "It's nothing against you. The cloth can't say whose fault a thing was. It can only say what was on the cart."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-155-"So we're worth less for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:"On the cloth. I never said the cloth was all of it." He looked at the fire for a breath, and then at his book, and he went on to the book, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "I've had that sheet thirty years. Forty-odd crews have been on it, one time and another, and gone off it. Some went home. Some the road had. There's not one of the forty that ever went over a line and came back to tell me so; there's none that I ever heard of who tried. I've a line in here now that says it was done." His finger lay on the leaf. "Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer. I'd a good look for the column to put that in. There isn't one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-159-"Then make one," said Lira.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-175----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:Pike unhooked it himself and laid it on the bench between his knee and the horn pot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-179-"You'll read it whether I give it you or not. You read the last one off my plank before I'd lifted the stone." He kept one finger on its corner. "It came up from the landing the morning after I'd said your line. The clerk brought it at a trot. His master had had it lying by since the same sack as the other, sealed up inside the same wrapper, with a bag counted and a word of direction on the outside: that it wasn't to be posted till the first was answered."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-181-"It was there all the time," said Brom. "The whole month that the other lay under your stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:183:"In the broker's strong-box, with his seal on it, and him not knowing what was in it. He says so. I believe him; he's not a man who'd keep three hundred marks shut up for a month if he knew that he had them." Pike took his finger away. "I said it to the meadow, twice. Nobody scratched. Then Teague came down off his fence for flour, and read it, and hung it where you found it. That's eight days it's been on my nail."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-185-Cael picked the paper up. It was the same weight as the other, which lay folded in Hesk's book; it had the same pricked margin, and one word standing alone at its head, with a stop after it. He held it so that the four of them could see, and read it out low, a stop at a time.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-191-Three hundred marks was more than twice what the five of them had earned in a hundred and twelve days. He did the sum without wishing to, and saw Brom doing it, with his lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:193:Pike had his eyes on his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-195-"Nothing else ever."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-197-"I'd not take it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:199:He did not raise his voice for it, and he kept his eyes on the book. He had told a man in this room, on the eighty-ninth evening, that he would have done the same with the first of those papers; and on the ninetieth he had held the first out across his plank to five people and written five strokes on it. He had not said then what he would do in their place. Cael understood that he had been given something that Pike did not give, and that it had not been asked for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-201-"The Stair road," said Lira. "Which is that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:"South. Past the well you cleared, and up onto the ridge. It's a carter's day to Millrace by it, and the road forks above the mill." Pike rubbed the stump of his arm through the pinned sleeve. "One branch drops to the ford. The other keeps the ridge half a day more, and comes off the end of it, and the carts go round what's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-204-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-205-"Round what?"
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-219-"Can it be read at our own fire?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:221:"It can be read where you like. It's been said." Pike pushed the paper an inch toward him along the bench. "Bring it back, or bring it marked. I'll be here till the fire's banked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-223-Cael folded it once, along the fold that it already had.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-1-# Chapter 58 — Thirty-Four
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-5-It was a plain enough place. The road divided at a heap of chock-stones that carters had left, and the right hand went on south and east under the foot of a long bare ridge. The left climbed away toward a notch between two hills, with the frost lying blue in it, and beyond the notch there was nothing to be seen but sky.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-29-She looked along the five of them then, once. Her sum ran to the oxen ford on the hundred and twenty-fifth, three weeks after it had looked for her; then she said the thing that she had stopped the mule to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-33-"I've been a stop on my own route for nine days. I'm going back to being the route."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md-61-He took out Hesk's book and wrote it standing, with the pencil cold in his fingers.
