editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-3-"Well?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-7-He knew what she meant, though he had been turned away at a bench three hours before, in six words, and had gone and sat down, and it had not once crossed his mind since that the matter might be opened again by her.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-11-"May I read you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:13:"Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it, and I want you to notice that it's the same day as the lean-to and a different question, and that the rule held both times. Put your hand on it. Surface first. Count of ten. Don't go in past the count until I say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-15-Lira had stopped chewing, and Karis, who had her notebook half out of her coat, pushed it back in. Cael had not stirred. There were two words in that answer which he could not make fit.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md-355-Cael sat down against the grey stone, in the full sun, and opened Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md-356-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md-358-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359:*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md-360-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md-361-He shut the book.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-305-They came off the wall on the side of the gap, one at a time, and did not stand. Seln had Cael's arm across his shoulders. Lira carried the roll, and Karis had her finished sheets held against her chest in both arms, with her face scraped raw along one cheek where the stone had had it. They went along the ledge, and over the sill, where the healer got up from her knees with Brom's weight on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-306-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:307:"I spent your week," said Brom. His face was the colour of the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-308-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md-309-"You spent it in a morning. I said that you would." She had her shoulder under his left arm, and she was wet to the waist. "Walk. You can be sorry on the grass."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-27-She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:"Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to" — and Brom said, "Arm," and she said, "No," and he didn't.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-31-"Twice."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-105-"There will be another edge," said Karis, to her. "I had a healer's count at the first. I should like one at the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:107:"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-109-Then her eyes went along the other four, and stopped at the sling.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-217-He stayed at the well until the grey had come into the east. Then he turned back sixty leaves, to a line that he had set down under an inn's eave on the forty-ninth night, when he had counted himself free. He read that through, and came forward again to the clean leaf, and wrote the one that had to stand across from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md-221-Behind him somebody was coming down the ladder, heavily, with a box.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md-179-"Then it's owed, and I've heard you say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:181:"Good." Oryn gave him the book. "Now lie down again. Not for me. You're the colour of the wall and you've been standing on that calf for a quarter of an hour, and I've just been told I'm a patient's source, and I'd like a minute to sit with the second thing before I go and eat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md-183-He lay down. She put the horn cup into his hand, with water in it, and waited until he had drunk. Then she took Hesk's book back and sat down on the three-legged stool herself, for the first time since he had come into that room, with the book on her knee.

