editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:147:"That's the Fallow Ring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:173:"I've walked past the Fallow Ring two hundred times. Never once stepped over the line. Thirty years on the roads and I've never met a practitioner who did it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:He turned round, since the others had. From the western ledge the whole bay lay open behind them. He could see the pale rock standing across its floor, and the two black lines above it that came to a point, and the wide scoured strip of the hill; and along the foot of the face, in a row, with a length and a half between each, the shale-backs were mowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:335:"It isn't there any longer," said Cael. "It went on the morning we came to it. There's a lip of rock thirty-one paces long that a mule can be led over, by somebody who knows how, and there's no road." He put his own finger on the margin, where Karis had written small. "And that's the herd. They're shale-backs, on the face over the bay. If anybody uses a Path beneath them, they run, and the hill comes with them, and that's how your cart went into the gorge. She's written that it's derived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:131:The ridge did not run down into a valley. It broke off, as a table breaks off at its edge, and its western face went down in one long slope of pale grass to a low place that lay below them like the print of an enormous thumb. It was a bowl, though so shallow that a cart might have been driven anywhere in it. He put it at five hundred yards from side to side, which was far wider than the swell that the Fallow Ring had stood on, and it had not half that swell's height.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:165:"Here," she said. "The edge of the Quieting. It is the same as the other; there is no warning in it at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:"I don't say that they have. I say that I know of one thing in this country that reaches every animal of a kind in the same instant, through rock, and it's the thing those hounds turned to. I'm not entitled to invent a second until I've used up the first." She drew a line across the new page. "Whatever a frightened shale-back gives off, the herd has a sense for it, and that's the same sense, or its cousin. Now look what they did with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:177:So she did not want the name of a place. There was a second reason for that, and it was on a sheet of thin paper in Seln's hand: a ring of charcoal, and beside it the memory of a sheet that had carried two. A one-armed man had said *a handful*. If she called this thing by the name of the Fallow Ring, then on the day that she stood at another she would have nothing to call that one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:191:*the Quieting*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how, and it did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:251:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:"A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:85:"Weather. It's cold on that top a month before it's cold here." Pike dipped his pen. "And shale-backs. The salt crews see them from the cold road, across the valley, on the high ledges. Bigger than a cart, and they eat moss."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:107:He knew what it would be. A word had been dropped at that plank a quarter of an hour ago, in a voice that was not quite Pike's ordinary one, and Seln collected such things as other men collected debts. He would be asking what a shale-back was, and who had been under one, and what had become of them, and he would get it in fewer sentences than anybody else could have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:123:"Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole." She rubbed her palms together. "He'd a name for it, the wold-wyrm; I'd not know a wold from a wash. He said that he'd not have gone down to those reeds for a year's salt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:141:"The edge of the Quieting," she said. "A third time, and no more warning in it than in the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:"I asked a question at the plank, two mornings ago, and I have carried the answer for a day and a half, since it concerned nothing that we had yet seen. I asked what a shale-back was, and whether a crew had ever come to harm by one." He inclined his head toward the horn. "He gave me the carters' rule first, and he gave it twice, and I shall give it as he did. 'Never be below them. Never be *loud* below them.'"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:237:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:43:They went the whole way round, with the Quieting on their right hand and four people watching from the rim.

