editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:181:The stream had spread as it reached the level. He saw the backs of perhaps a dozen animals, wheeling on the bay's floor in a fog of dust, with the shale still running between their legs like water; and beyond them, alone, in the place where five people had stood a minute ago, the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
