editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:73:"You'll have a minute of the long one," she said. "No more. I'll count it, and I'll say when, and you'll come out on my word and not on your own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:77:"I said that I don't run it on a road, since it costs me a mending and I've none to throw about. It's you that's running this, and I'm only sitting under it." She was turning her collar down. "And a minute's no reading. It's a look through a door. But the short one shows you that there's a bed, and it never yet showed anybody what was running in it. I'd not have you go back to yourself knowing the half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:97:That was the difference, and it took him some of his minute to find the word for it. His own went along, quick or dragging, as he was glad or tired, and it had never seemed to him to be going anywhere in particular. Hers was going somewhere: it ran as a river runs in its last mile, when it can smell the sea. Wherever his attention rested on it, he found it already leaning that way, toward his left hand, and then toward his right; it came to meet whatever touched it. It did not pool in any place, and there was no slack water in her at all. The whole of that woman, from the neck to the heels, was on its way *to* something, and had been for so long that the banks had worn to fit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:277:*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
